    "math_academy_id": "q-87910",
    "knowledge_point_id": "a7656d23-06c9-5d22-b62a-3636bca66ab6",
    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Base or the Height of a Triangle Given Its Area",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945897/assets/4c19425664f79df1f46863f59268ebd27e20defce41b21e877926bbcadc9e5c3.png)\n\nConsider the triangles $△ABC$ and $△CDE$ shown above. If the area of $△CDE$ is $42{\\text{ cm}}^{2}$, find the area of $△ABC.$\n\n(Figure not drawn to scale)",
    "worked_solution": "Let's consider $△CDE.$ We can see that $\\overset{―}{CD}$ is the height corresponding to the base $\\overset{―}{DE}$ and that $DE=7\\text{ cm}.$ \n\nSince the area of $△CDE$ is $42{\\text{ cm}}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\ 42 & =\\frac{7\\cdot CD}{2} \\\\ 7CD & =84 \\\\ CD & =12\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that $\\overset{―}{CD}$ is also the height of $△ABC$ corresponding to the base $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945897/assets/0014faf05eaf15d70f885802e5b820b0c72d42a96f400442f827ed229de8b240.png)\n\nTherefore, we find the area of $△ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{AB\\cdot CD}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 12}{2} \\\\  & =30{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
